    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d249">

                <pb facs="0575" n="439" sameAs="frus:frus1929v01/pg439"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">711.554/2: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Belgium</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Gibson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Brussels</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1929-01-18T13:00:00+00:00">January 18, 1929—1
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received 3 p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>7. Department’s instruction No. 167, December 1, 1928.<anchor n="2"
                        xml:id="d249-f1"/> Foreign Office asks for information as soon as possible
                    whether American laws contain any provision which would permit children born in
                    the United States of a Belgian father, even if they continue to reside
                    permanently in the United States, to renounce American nationality on arriving
                    at a certain age and to retain only the nationality of the father.</p>
                <p>It seems to me that by virtue of section 2 of the act [of] March 2, 1907,<anchor
                        n="3" xml:id="d249-f2"/> an oath of allegiance to the Belgian Government
                    would be sufficient to divest the child of American citizenship.</p>
                <p>Please instruct.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Gibson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
